DevOps Interview Preparation Series — Understanding HTTP Status Code Families (1xx–5xx) 🚀 

Every DevOps or SRE engineer should know how to interpret HTTP response codes, especially when troubleshooting APIs, load balancers, or microservices in production.

Today, let’s decode one of the most common interview topics —
 HTTP Status Code Families — and understand their meanings, causes, and real-world solutions 👇

1xx — Informational Responses
These codes mean the server has received the request and the process is continuing.
Common Codes:
100 Continue → The client can continue sending the request body.
101 Switching Protocols → The server is switching protocols (like HTTP to WebSocket).
Use Case:
 Seen in APIs or streaming connections where headers are sent before the full request body.

2xx — Success Responses
These indicate the request was successfully received, understood, and processed.
Common Codes:
200 OK → Request successful (GET/POST completed).
201 Created → Resource successfully created (POST requests).
202 Accepted → Request accepted but not yet processed.
204 No Content → Success but no response body returned.

3xx — Redirection Responses
These codes tell the client that additional actions are needed to complete the request.
Common Codes:
301 Moved Permanently → Resource permanently moved to a new URL.
302 Found → Temporary redirection.
304 Not Modified → Cached resource; no need to download again.


4xx — Client Errors
Indicates the client made an error (bad request, unauthorized access, or missing resources).
Common Codes & Fixes:
400 Bad Request → Invalid syntax or malformed request.
Fix: Validate input data before sending.
401 Unauthorized → Missing or invalid authentication.
Fix: Add or refresh access tokens/credentials.
403 Forbidden → Authenticated but no permission.
Fix: Check IAM, ACL, or API gateway access policies.
404 Not Found → Resource not found.
Fix: Verify API endpoint or DNS configuration.
429 Too Many Requests → Rate limit exceeded.
Fix: Add retry logic or increase rate limits.

5xx — Server Errors
These codes mean the server failed to process the request, often due to misconfigurations or internal crashes.
Common Codes & Fixes:
500 Internal Server Error → General backend failure.
Fix: Check logs, code errors, or backend dependency failures.
502 Bad Gateway → Invalid response from upstream server.
Fix: Check load balancer target health or backend timeout.
503 Service Unavailable → Server overloaded or under maintenance.
Fix: Scale up instances, check auto-scaling, or increase timeouts.
504 Gateway Timeout → Upstream server didn’t respond in time.
Fix: Tune backend performance or adjust timeout settings.
